or_of_Justin_1964_hardcover_dust_jacket.png)\n\n***The Rector of Justin*** is a 1964 [psychological 
fiction](/wiki/Psychological_fiction \"Psychological fiction\") novel by [Louis Auchincloss](/wiki/Louis_Auchincloss 
\"Louis Auchincloss\") about the headmaster (or \"[rector](/wiki/Rector_(ecclesiastical) \"Rector (ecclesiastical)\")\")
of a socially exclusive American [boarding school](/wiki/Boarding_school \"Boarding school\"). Over the decades, various
narrators provide contrasting perspectives on rector Francis Prescott's charismatic personality and autocratic 
leadership style. Through the narrators' disagreements, the novel gradually unveils that [White Anglo-Saxon 
Protestant](/wiki/White_Anglo-Saxon_Protestants \"White Anglo-Saxon Protestants\") society—of which Prescott is a 
reluctant mascot—has lost its innocence and abandoned its Christian values.\n\nThe novel was a commercial hit and was 
warmly received by most contemporary critics. It was a finalist for the [Pulitzer Prize for 
Fiction](/wiki/Pulitzer_Prize_for_Fiction \"Pulitzer Prize for Fiction\") and the [National Book Award for 
Fiction](/wiki/National_Book_Award_for_Fiction \"National Book Award for Fiction\"), losing to [Shirley Ann 
Grau](/wiki/Shirley_Ann_Grau \"Shirley Ann Grau\")'s *[The Keeper of the House](/wiki/The_Keepers_of_the_House \"The 
Keepers of the House\")* and [Saul Bellow](/wiki/Saul_Bellow \"Saul Bellow\")'s *[Herzog](/wiki/Herzog_(novel) \"Herzog 
