"title": "The Rector of Justin — Grokipedia", "content": "*The Rector of Justin* is a 1964 psychological novel by 
American author Louis Auchincloss, chronicling the life and character of Reverend Frank Prescott, the founder and 
long-serving rector of Justin Martyr, a fictional elite Episcopal boys' preparatory school in rural New 
England.](#ref-1)](#ref-2) The narrative unfolds through the retrospective accounts of six narrators—including former 
students, colleagues, and family members—who offer contrasting perspectives on Prescott's authoritarian leadership, 
personal hypocrisies, and unyielding commitment to classical education and Christian moral rigor, revealing a man both 
inspiring and flawed.](#ref-3) Auchincloss, drawing from his own experiences at Groton School and his background in 
Manhattan's upper echelons, uses the novel to probe themes of power, faith, and institutional tradition amid 
mid-20th-century social shifts, portraying Prescott as a domineering figure whose ideals clash with evolving societal 
norms and personal scandals.](#ref-4) Regarded as one of Auchincloss's finest works, it has been lauded for its incisive
character study and elegant prose, with critics highlighting Prescott as a complex archetype of American elite 
authority—capable of pettiness and self-dramatization yet driven by genuine conviction.", "score": 0.7244226, 
