    "raw_content_or_summary": "This GradeSaver page presents essay questions for *The Rector of Justin* along with 
sample responses. The notes are community-contributed.\n\nOne question asks why Prescott is so unwavering in building a 
boys’ boarding school. The answer explains that Prescott was educated at a boys’ boarding school founded by Horace, a 
religious fanatic who instilled discipline and inspired his students. Prescott became close to Horace and promised to 
follow in his footsteps by establishing his own boys’ boarding school. The response emphasizes that Prescott grows up 
believing he has a mission, and his determination and interest in empowering boys drive him to fulfill this goal, 
suggesting deep ideological commitment to single-sex education and moral formation of young men.\n\nAnother question 
explores the relationship between Prescott and his daughter Cordelia. The answer portrays Prescott as a strict father 
devoted to his own ideologies and long-standing dream of founding a boys’ school, despite having only daughters. 
Cordelia is depicted as rebellious and bold, frequently challenging her father when she disagrees. She is angered by his
decision to focus on a boys’ school rather than programs to empower his daughters and other girls, viewing this as 
favoritism toward men. The commentary notes that Cordelia even speculates her father might be gay because his emotional 
focus seems to be on men and boys rather than women, highlighting themes of gender, patriarchy, and emotional 
orientation.\n\nA third question discusses why the school board forces Prescott to retire as headmaster. The answer 
stresses Prescott’s strong attachment to the Episcopal school he built with his own resources and has led since its 
founding. However, at age 82, his efficiency has declined, in movement and thinking, raising concerns among the board. 
The board, having authority to remove the head, compels his retirement so that a younger, more energetic man named David
(described as taking over the position) can lead the school. The response frames the board’s actions as motivated by the
school’s best interests while also recognizing Prescott’s reluctance to relinquish control.\n\nThe page includes a 
prompt inviting users to update or revise the section and links to the broader study guide (background information, 
summary, character list, glossary, themes, etc.). The essay answers collectively underscore key themes: devotion to 
educational ideals, gender tensions within the family, generational change and leadership succession, and the 
institutional governance of Justin Martyr."
