      "local_ref": "e5",
      "text": "The New York Times obituary of Louis Auchincloss, who died in 2010, said, \"'The Rector of Justin' 
(1964), which was a best seller and a finalist for the National Book Award, is regarded by many critics as Mr. 
Auchincloss’s best and most important novel. Its protagonist is Frank Prescott, the headmaster of a New England boys’ 
school before the war, a man of intellect and idealism who could be noble, generous and kind but also, by turns, cruel, 
callous and arbitrary.\"",
      "document_ref": "d3",
      "source_ref": "s3",
      "evidence_type": "quote",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": ["secondary quotation of NYT obituary"],
      "limitations": ["obituary is quoted indirectly; original article not directly consulted here"],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e6",
      "text": "At one point the Prescott character in the book says, \"The older I get the more I realize that the only 
thing a teacher has to go on is that rare spark in a boy’s eye.\"",
      "document_ref": "d3",
      "source_ref": "s3",
      "evidence_type": "quote",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": ["dialogue_from_novel"],
      "limitations": ["page and chapter not specified"],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e7",
      "text": "Later in The Rector of Justin, the Prescott character says, \"There is no real distinction between the 
pulpit and the classroom. I tried to put God into every book and sport in Justin. That was my ideal, to spread a sense 
of his presence so that it would not be confined to prayers and sacred studies and to spread it in such a way as to make
the school joyful.\"",
      "document_ref": "d3",
      "source_ref": "s3",
      "evidence_type": "quote",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": ["dialogue_from_novel"],
      "limitations": ["page and chapter not specified"],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e8",
      "text": "Elsewhere in the book, Prescott claims, \"the greatness of the private school…is not that it produces 
geniuses—they grow anyway, and can’t be made—but that it can sometimes turn a third-rate student into a second-rate 
one.\"",
      "document_ref": "d3",
      "source_ref": "s3",
      "evidence_type": "quote",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": ["dialogue_from_novel"],
      "limitations": ["ellipsis indicates omitted text; full sentence not shown"],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e9",
      "text": "Louis Auchincloss was the author of more than sixty books of fiction in a writing career that spanned the
second half of the twentieth century and the opening years of the twenty-first. He was also an estate lawyer for a 
sizable portion of that time and a denizen of a privileged sphere of New York society.",
      "document_ref": "d4",
      "source_ref": "s4",
      "evidence_type": "quote",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": ["author_background"],
      "limitations": ["blog commentary rather than a formal biography"],
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e10",
      "text": "Yet among his extensive body of work there remain standout performances. Auchincloss himself told George 
Plimpton that he would like to be remembered on the strength of three books, Portrait in Brownstone, House of Five 
Talents, and The Rector of Justin, which was a finalist for the National Book Award in 1965. It lost out, deservedly, to
Saul Bellow’s Herzog.",
      "document_ref": "d4",
      "source_ref": "s4",
      "evidence_type": "quote",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": ["award_history", "author_self-assessment"],
      "limitations": ["citation of Plimpton interview is indirect"],
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e11",
      "text": "The rector of Justin is Reverend Francis Prescott, D.D., founder and headmaster of Justin Martyr, a 
school modeled on Auchincloss’s years as a student at Groton.",
      "document_ref": "d4",
      "source_ref": "s4",
      "evidence_type": "quote",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": ["character_identification", "school_model_inference"],
      "limitations": ["link to Groton is presented as the reviewer's interpretation"],
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e12",
      "text": "He is a great man in the eyes of his new hire, a young master by the name of Brian Aspinwall, who 
provides early impressions via his journal. Aspinwall is undeniably callow, and wracked with self-doubt, but he views 
Prescott as a pole star of sorts, a man whose example he will never be able to credibly follow, but whose very existence
justifies his own efforts to live as a man of faith and guide his young charges as ably as possible.",
      "document_ref": "d4",
      "source_ref": "s4",
      "evidence_type": "quote",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": ["plot_summary", "characterization"],
      "limitations": ["partly interpretive language by the reviewer"],
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e13",
      "text": "Charley Strong, a former Justin Martyr who eventually ends up in a romantic relationship with Prescott’s 
daughter, writes in the aftermath of his World War I service that, \"He it was who baptized and confirmed me, he who 
talked to me of my doubts and miseries, he who gave me a love that made the shallow, prattling love of shallow, 
prattling parents seem like the spray on one’s face in a speedboat at sea.\"",
      "document_ref": "d4",
      "source_ref": "s4",
      "evidence_type": "quote",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": ["character_speech", "relationship_details"],
      "limitations": ["quotation is selective; context in the novel is not fully given"],
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e14",
      "text": "Early on, Aspinwall notes that he would like to use his newly started journal to keep a record of 
Prescott’s last years at Justin Martyr. Gradually the idea emerges – not of his own accord – that he should write 
Prescott’s life. He consults Horace Havistock’s account of a youth spent as Prescott’s great friend, but this is prior 
to David Griscam’s conclusion that, since Prescott won’t cooperate with his idea to write the headmaster’s life, 
Aspinwall should do so.",
      "document_ref": "d4",
      "source_ref": "s4",
      "evidence_type": "quote",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": ["plot_development", "narrative_structure"],
      "limitations": ["summary language interwoven with paraphrase"],
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e15",
      "text": "The Rector of Justin  Louis Auchincloss  352 pages, Paperback  First published January 1, 1964",
      "document_ref": "d5",
      "source_ref": "s5",
      "evidence_type": "data",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": ["Goodreads_metadata"],
      "limitations": ["community-generated data; first publication date may be approximate"],
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "e16",
      "text": "The Rector of Justin, originally published in 1964, is widely considered to be his most ambitious novel 
and his greatest achievement.",
      "document_ref": "d6",
      "source_ref": "s6",
      "evidence_type": "quote",
      "locator": null,
      "qualifiers": ["bookseller_description", "critical_reputation_summary"],
      "limitations": ["reflects the bookseller's summary of critical opinion; specific critics not named"],
      "confidence": "medium"
    }
  ],
  "conflicts": [
