the novel, author, year of publication, publisher, genre, and general significance; summarize the setting and narrative 
structure; mention major themes and reception, all in a neutral tone.\n- Coverage: Include bibliographic details (first 
edition publisher, year, format, approximate page count, notable later editions and ISBN where available), basic author 
background relevant to the novel, concise plot overview, main characters and their roles, thematic analysis drawn from 
secondary sources, and a summary of contemporary and later critical reception (including major reviews and scholarly 
commentary) supported by the evidence in the graph.\n- Neutrality and words-to-watch: Avoid promotional language, 
unsourced opinions, and weasel words; attribute interpretive or evaluative statements to named sources or clearly 
described critical viewpoints; indicate relative prominence of views when multiple interpretations exist.\n- Citations: 
Every factual claim (dates, names, plot facts, thematic claims, reception statements, etc.) must be followed immediately
by a bracketed-number citation like [1], [2]. Re-use numbers consistently when the same source supports multiple 
sentences.\n- References section: At the end, include a 'References' heading and then a numbered list where each number 
corresponds to one of the in-text citations, giving a full URL for each source. Do not include or rely on any 
en.wikipedia.org URLs.\n\nThe article should be concise but complete enough to satisfy the defined objectives and style 
constraints. Do not mention the Research Graph, tools, or your own role; write as if you were a standard Wikipedia 
article editor."})
