Characters, Themes, Reception and legacy, and References.
- Lead: Identify the novel, author, year of publication, publisher, genre, and general significance; summarize the 
setting and narrative structure; mention major themes and reception, all in a neutral tone.
- Coverage: Include bibliographic details (first edition publisher, year, format, approximate page count, notable later 
editions and ISBN where available), basic author background relevant to the novel, concise plot overview, main 
characters and their roles, thematic analysis drawn from secondary sources, and a summary of contemporary and later 
critical reception (including major reviews and scholarly commentary) supported by the evidence in the graph.
- Neutrality and words-to-watch: Avoid promotional language, unsourced opinions, and weasel words; attribute 
interpretive or evaluative statements to named sources or clearly described critical viewpoints; indicate relative 
prominence of views when multiple interpretations exist.
- Citations: Every factual claim (dates, names, plot facts, thematic claims, reception statements, etc.) must be 
followed immediately by a bracketed-number citation like [1], [2]. Re-use numbers consistently when the same source 
supports multiple sentences.
- References section: At the end, include a 'References' heading and then a numbered list where each number corresponds 
to one of the in-text citations, giving a full URL for each source. Do not include or rely on any en.wikipedia.org URLs.

The article should be concise but complete enough to satisfy the defined objectives and style constraints. Do not 
mention the Research Graph, tools, or your own role; write as if you were a standard Wikipedia article editor.
