"published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260702014143505337_244ff918", "type": "Evidence", "text": "\"September 10, 1939. I 
have always wanted to keep a journal, but whenever I am about to start one, I am dissuaded by the idea that it is too 
late. … I shall be an instructor of English at Justin Martyr, an Episcopal boys' boarding school thirty miles west of 
Boston.\"", "source_ids": ["20260702014141244249_4b619dd3"], "citations": [5], "sources": [{"source_id": 
"20260702014141244249_4b619dd3", "title": "The Rector of Justin: A Novel (AbeBooks / Harper Perennial edition)", "url": 
"https://www.abebooks.com/9780618224890/Rector-Justin-Novel-Auchincloss-Louis-0618224890/plp", "source_type": "other", 
"published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260702014143589375_75e8504d", "type": "Evidence", "text": "A CRUCIBLE COVERED WITH 
IVY; THE RECTOR OF JUSTIN. By Louis Auchincloss. 341 pp. Boston: Houghton Mifflin Company. $4.95.", "source_ids": 
["20260702014141284253_4b6ddc1b"], "citations": [6], "sources": [{"source_id": "20260702014141284253_4b6ddc1b", "title":
"A CRUCIBLE COVERED WITH IVY; THE RECTOR OF JUSTIN. By Louis Auchincloss.", "url": 
"https://www.nytimes.com/1964/07/12/archives/a-crucible-covered-with-ivy-the-rector-of-justin-by-louis.html", 
"source_type": "news", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260702014143682237_084ebb02", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The
New York Times obituary of Louis Auchincloss, who died in 2010, said, \"'The Rector of Justin' (1964), which was a best 
seller and a finalist for the National Book Award, is regarded by many critics as Mr. Auchincloss’s best and most 
important novel. Its protagonist is Frank Prescott, the headmaster of a New England boys’ school before the war, a man 
of intellect and idealism who could be noble, generous and kind but also, by turns, cruel, callous and arbitrary.\"", 
"source_ids": ["20260702014141319177_072ddec6"], "citations": [7], "sources": [{"source_id": 
"20260702014141319177_072ddec6", "title": "From a Half-Century-Old Novel, Wisdom about Teaching and Education Reform, 
with 2020 Political Implications", "url": 
"https://www.educationnext.org/from-half-century-old-novel-wisdom-about-teaching-education-reform-with-2020-political-im
plications-auchincloss", "source_type": "report", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260702014143773858_dae04afc", 
"type": "Evidence", "text": "At one point the Prescott character in the book says, \"The older I get the more I realize 
that the only thing a teacher has to go on is that rare spark in a boy’s eye.\"", "source_ids": 
["20260702014141319177_072ddec6"], "citations": [7], "sources": [{"source_id": "20260702014141319177_072ddec6", "title":
"From a Half-Century-Old Novel, Wisdom about Teaching and Education Reform, with 2020 Political Implications", "url": 
"https://www.educationnext.org/from-half-century-old-novel-wisdom-about-teaching-education-reform-with-2020-political-im
plications-auchincloss", "source_type": "report", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260702014143851824_9fa01b8a", 
"type": "Evidence", "text": "Later in The Rector of Justin, the Prescott character says, \"There is no real distinction 
between the pulpit and the classroom. I tried to put God into every book and sport in Justin. That was my ideal, to 
spread a sense of his presence so that it would not be confined to prayers and sacred studies and to spread it in such a
way as to make the school joyful.\"", "source_ids": ["20260702014141319177_072ddec6"], "citations": [7], "sources": 
[{"source_id": "20260702014141319177_072ddec6", "title": "From a Half-Century-Old Novel, Wisdom about Teaching and 
Education Reform, with 2020 Political Implications", "url": 
"https://www.educationnext.org/from-half-century-old-novel-wisdom-about-teaching-education-reform-with-2020-political-im
plications-auchincloss", "source_type": "report", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260702014143938625_5b8e5036", 
"type": "Evidence", "text": "Elsewhere in the book, Prescott claims, \"the greatness of the private school…is not that 
it produces geniuses—they grow anyway, and can’t be made—but that it can sometimes turn a third-rate student into a 
second-rate one.\"", "source_ids": ["20260702014141319177_072ddec6"], "citations": [7], "sources": [{"source_id": 
"20260702014141319177_072ddec6", "title": "From a Half-Century-Old Novel, Wisdom about Teaching and Education Reform, 
with 2020 Political Implications", "url": 
"https://www.educationnext.org/from-half-century-old-novel-wisdom-about-teaching-education-reform-with-2020-political-im
plications-auchincloss", "source_type": "report", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260702014144022400_f72498fa", 
"type": "Evidence", "text": "Louis Auchincloss was the author of more than sixty books of fiction in a writing career 
that spanned the second half of the twentieth century and the opening years of the twenty-first. He was also an estate 
lawyer for a sizable portion of that time and a denizen of a privileged sphere of New York society.", "source_ids": 
["20260702014141354511_38355e9f"], "citations": [8], "sources": [{"source_id": "20260702014141354511_38355e9f", "title":
"The Rector of Justin by Louis Auchincloss", "url": 
"https://goodreadingcopy.wordpress.com/2013/09/02/the-rector-of-justin-by-louis-auchincloss", "source_type": "blog", 
"published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260702014144109598_040d76a8", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Yet among his extensive 
body of work there remain standout performances. Auchincloss himself told George Plimpton that he would like to be 
remembered on the strength of three books, Portrait in Brownstone, House of Five Talents, and The Rector of Justin, 
which was a finalist for the National Book Award in 1965. It lost out, deservedly, to Saul Bellow’s Herzog.", 
"source_ids": ["20260702014141354511_38355e9f"], "citations": [8], "sources": [{"source_id": 
"20260702014141354511_38355e9f", "title": "The Rector of Justin by Louis Auchincloss", "url": 
"https://goodreadingcopy.wordpress.com/2013/09/02/the-rector-of-justin-by-louis-auchincloss", "source_type": "blog", 
"published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260702014144202322_3458cf73", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The rector of Justin is 
Reverend Francis Prescott, D.D., founder and headmaster of Justin Martyr, a school modeled on Auchincloss’s years as a 
student at Groton.", "source_ids": ["20260702014141354511_38355e9f"], "citations": [8], "sources": [{"source_id": 
"20260702014141354511_38355e9f", "title": "The Rector of Justin by Louis Auchincloss", "url": 
"https://goodreadingcopy.wordpress.com/2013/09/02/the-rector-of-justin-by-louis-auchincloss", "source_type": "blog", 
"published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260702014144290326_90f30e7e", "type": "Evidence", "text": "He is a great man in the 
eyes of his new hire, a young master by the name of Brian Aspinwall, who provides early impressions via his journal. 
Aspinwall is undeniably callow, and wracked with self-doubt, but he views Prescott as a pole star of sorts, a man whose 
example he will never be able to credibly follow, but whose very existence justifies his own efforts to live as a man of
faith and guide his young charges as ably as possible.", "source_ids": ["20260702014141354511_38355e9f"], "citations": 
[8], "sources": [{"source_id": "20260702014141354511_38355e9f", "title": "The Rector of Justin by Louis Auchincloss", 
"url": "https://goodreadingcopy.wordpress.com/2013/09/02/the-rector-of-justin-by-louis-auchincloss", "source_type": 
"blog", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260702014144376778_63268a45", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Charley Strong, a 
former Justin Martyr who eventually ends up in a romantic relationship with Prescott’s daughter, writes in the aftermath
of his World War I service that, \"He it was who baptized and confirmed me, he who talked to me of my doubts and 
miseries, he who gave me a love that made the shallow, prattling love of shallow, prattling parents seem like the spray 
on one’s face in a speedboat at sea.\"", "source_ids": ["20260702014141354511_38355e9f"], "citations": [8], "sources": 
[{"source_id": "20260702014141354511_38355e9f", "title": "The Rector of Justin by Louis Auchincloss", "url": 
"https://goodreadingcopy.wordpress.com/2013/09/02/the-rector-of-justin-by-louis-auchincloss", "source_type": "blog", 
"published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260702014144458736_c48c044e", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Early on, Aspinwall notes 
that he would like to use his newly started journal to keep a record of Prescott’s last years at Justin Martyr. 
Gradually the idea emerges – not of his own accord – that he should write Prescott’s life. He consults Horace 
Havistock’s account of a youth spent as Prescott’s great friend, but this is prior to David Griscam’s conclusion that, 
since Prescott won’t cooperate with his idea to write the headmaster’s life, Aspinwall should do so.", "source_ids": 
["20260702014141354511_38355e9f"], "citations": [8], "sources": [{"source_id": "20260702014141354511_38355e9f", "title":
"The Rector of Justin by Louis Auchincloss", "url": 
"https://goodreadingcopy.wordpress.com/2013/09/02/the-rector-of-justin-by-louis-auchincloss", "source_type": "blog", 
"published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260702014144544498_e1d10f5d", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The Rector of Justin  
Louis Auchincloss  352 pages, Paperback  First published January 1, 1964", "source_ids": 
["20260702014141392725_1dc653e8"], "citations": [9], "sources": [{"source_id": "20260702014141392725_1dc653e8", "title":
"The Rector of Justin (Goodreads entry)", "url": "https://www.goodreads.com/en/book/show/79669.The_Rector_of_Justin", 
