[tools:9ff2a85e-0759-629c-79f6-e2333b8f39a8] [AIMessage → tool] commit_report_chapter({'chapter_id': 2, 'title': 'Plot 
and characters', 'section_type': 'body', 'content': 'Plot\n\nThe Rector of Justin follows the life and career of 
Reverend Frank (Francis) Prescott, founder and long‑serving rector of Justin Martyr, an Episcopal boys’ boarding school 
in rural New England about thirty miles west of Boston.[3][5][7][8] Within the novel’s fictional biography, Prescott is 
born in 1860, loses his father in the American Civil War, and is educated at Oxford University, where he absorbs ideals 
of classical British education that he later adapts in shaping upper‑class American boys into moral and intellectual 
leaders.[3] Study‑guide materials further interpret his unwavering commitment to creating a boys’ boarding school as 
rooted in his formative experience at a similar institution founded by Horace, a religiously zealous headmaster whose 
discipline and inspiration Prescott internalizes.[2]\n\nPrescott establishes Justin Martyr in the early twentieth 
century, transforming it from a struggling school into a prestigious New England preparatory institution through strict 
discipline, intellectual rigor, and moral guidance.[1][3] Over the decades, his interactions with students portray him 
as both a demanding mentor and a father figure whose influence shapes their lives and prospects, while his adherence to 
tradition generates controversy and clashes with some pupils and colleagues.[1][7] The novel’s timeline spans roughly 
eighty years of Prescott’s life and explicitly integrates major historical events such as the Great Depression and the 
Second World War as forces that affect the school and its community.[1][3][4]\n\nThe narrative begins in autumn 1939, 
when Prescott is around eighty, and is non‑linear, assembled from diaries, memoirs, letters, and interviews that reflect
on his past and present.[3][4][5] The primary frame narrator is Brian Aspinwall, a twenty‑seven‑year‑old English 
instructor and former Justin Martyr student, who arrives at the school just as war in Europe begins and starts a journal
dated 10 September 1939 to record his impressions.[3][5][8] Initially aspiring simply to chronicle Prescott’s final 
years, Aspinwall is drawn into the project of writing the rector’s life, partly through his consultation of Horace 
Havistock’s account of Prescott’s youth and partly at the prompting of David Griscam, a former student and lawyer who 
suggests Aspinwall undertake the biography after Prescott refuses to cooperate with Griscam’s own 
attempt.[3][8]\n\nThrough Aspinwall’s journal and the inserted testimonies of other narrators, the reader encounters key
episodes such as Prescott’s efforts to maintain the school’s religious and classical curriculum, conflicts with the 
governing board over finances and modernization, and mounting pressure for him to retire as age diminishes his physical 
and mental agility.[1][2][3] Study‑guide analyses describe how the board ultimately forces Prescott to step down around 
age eighty‑two, appointing a younger man named David to succeed him despite Prescott’s strong attachment to the 
institution he built and his reluctance to relinquish authority.[2] The concluding sections of the novel draw together 
the multiple perspectives to present a complex image of Prescott as a man whose ideals have shaped generations of 
students but whose personal compromises and authoritarian style leave mixed legacies for his family and the 
school.[1][3][7]\n\nCharacters\n\nFrank (Francis) Prescott is the central figure of the novel, depicted as the 
influential headmaster and rector of Justin Martyr School whose life and work form the focus of the 
narrative.[1][3][4][5] Reference sources and critical essays describe him as an intellectual and idealist who can be 
noble, generous, and kind, yet also cruel, callous, and arbitrary in his exercise of authority.[7][8] Within the story, 
Prescott articulates an educational philosophy that stresses both the limitations and possibilities of schooling, 
asserting that the greatness of a private school is not in producing geniuses but in sometimes raising a third‑rate 
student to second‑rate, and that a teacher’s only reliable guide is the "rare spark" in a boy’s eye.[7] He also insists 
that there is "no real distinction between the pulpit and the classroom," explaining that he has tried to put God "into 
every book and sport" at Justin so that religious faith permeates the school’s life and makes it joyful rather than 
confined to formal worship.[7]\n\nBrian Aspinwall serves as the primary frame narrator and one of the six major voices 
through which the story is told.[3][4][5] He is introduced as a young and initially timid English instructor, previously
a student at Justin, who regards Prescott as a model of spiritual leadership and sees his own efforts as derivative by 
comparison.[3][5][8] Aspinwall’s journals provide early impressions of the school’s routines, the rector’s personality, 
and the tensions between tradition and change, and his evolving biographical project structures much of the novel’s 
narrative.[3][5]\n\nHorace Havistock appears as Prescott’s contemporary from his youth and Oxford years.[3][8] His 
memoir or diary material recounts their friendship and the educational ideals that shaped Prescott, including exposure 
to rigorous religious discipline at a boys’ boarding school that informs Prescott’s later decision to found Justin 
Martyr.[2][3] David Griscam, a former student of the school who becomes a lawyer and later chair of the board, provides 
another narrative strand that highlights institutional governance and the conflict between Prescott’s personal authority
and the board’s responsibility.[3][8] Study‑guide commentary notes that when Prescott refuses to cooperate with 
Griscam’s plan to write his biography, Griscam encourages Aspinwall to undertake the task instead.[8]\n\nJules Griscam, 
David’s son, contributes a more critical and emotionally charged perspective shaped by personal tragedy connected to the
school and to Prescott’s decisions.[3] Cordelia Prescott, the rector’s youngest daughter, is portrayed in study‑guide 
analyses as rebellious and outspoken, often challenging her father’s focus on a boys’ school and criticizing what she 
perceives as his favoritism toward men and boys at the expense of opportunities for his daughters and other women.[2][3]
One interpretive essay describes Cordelia as speculating that her father might be gay because his emotional attention 
appears primarily directed toward male students and colleagues rather than toward women.[2]\n\nCharley Strong is 
presented as a favored former pupil of Justin Martyr who later becomes romantically involved with Prescott’s 
daughter.[3][8] His retrospective account emphasizes Prescott’s role in his spiritual development, describing the 
rector’s guidance and affection as exceeding that of conventional parental love and reinforcing the novel’s portrayal of
Prescott as both a spiritual father figure and a potentially overbearing authority.[8] Together, these characters and 
narrators contribute overlapping and sometimes conflicting portraits of Prescott, his family, and the institution, 
underscoring the multi‑voiced structure of the novel.\n', 'source_ids': ['20260702013901026585_d92db8f0', 
