[tools:9ff2a85e-0759-629c-79f6-e2333b8f39a8] [AIMessage → tool] submit_final_report({'title': 'The Rector of Justin', 
'sections': [{'title': 'Lead and background', 'content': 'The Rector of Justin is a psychological novel by American 
author Louis Auchincloss, first published in 1964 by Houghton Mifflin in Boston.[3][6][9][10] The original U.S. 
hardcover edition ran to approximately 341 pages and was priced at US$4.95.[6] The novel centers on Reverend Frank 
(Francis) Prescott, founder and long‑serving rector of Justin Martyr, an elite Episcopal boys’ preparatory school in 
rural New England, and traces roughly eighty years of his life and career.[3][5][7][8] Its non‑linear narrative is 
constructed from the retrospective accounts of six narrators, including the young English instructor Brian Aspinwall, 
who together offer contrasting perspectives on Prescott’s character, educational ideals, and the boarding school he 
created.[3][4][5][8] The book has been described in reference sources and later commentary as one of Auchincloss’s most 
accomplished and ambitious novels, a bestseller and a finalist for the 1965 National Book Award, and it has been cited 
as the work for which many critics regard him best known.[3][7][8][10] Critical discussions have emphasized its 
exploration of power, faith, institutional tradition, and mid‑twentieth‑century social change within the setting of an 
American elite boarding school.[3][7]\n\nBackground and publication history\n\nThe Rector of Justin was first issued in 
hardcover by Houghton Mifflin in 1964, in an edition of about 341 pages priced at US$4.95.[6] Later bibliographic 
sources list the work as having 352 pages, reflecting the pagination of subsequent paperback printings.[9] A trade 
paperback edition was published by Harper Perennial (associated with Mariner Books) in 2002 under ISBN 0‑618‑22489‑0 
(978‑0‑618‑22489‑0).[5] Bookseller and catalog descriptions from later decades identify the novel as one of 
Auchincloss’s major works and note continued circulation in reprint form.[5][10]\n\nLouis Auchincloss, a New York 
attorney and member of an elite social milieu, wrote more than sixty works of fiction during a career that spanned the 
latter half of the twentieth century and the early twenty‑first century.[8] According to a later critical essay, he told
interviewer George Plimpton that among his novels he most wished to be remembered for Portrait in Brownstone, House of 
Five Talents, and The Rector of Justin.[8] An Education Next essay quoting The New York Times obituary for Auchincloss 
notes that The Rector of Justin was both a bestseller and a finalist for the National Book Award, and that many critics 
regard it as his best and most important novel.[7]\n\nCommentators have linked the fictional Justin Martyr school to 
Auchincloss’s own experience as a student at Groton School, a prominent New England boys’ boarding institution, 
describing the novel’s rector, Reverend Francis Prescott, as modeled on that milieu.[8] Reference and study guides 
characterize the novel as a psychological study of Prescott as an archetype of American upper‑class authority, examining
his mixture of idealism, religious conviction, and authoritarian leadership in the context of a changing 
mid‑twentieth‑century United States.[3][7]\n', 'source_ids': ['20260702014141244249_4b619dd3', 
