 • The GradeSaver community note summary describes The Rector of Justin as following the life and career of Frank       
   Prescott, the influential headmaster (rector) of the prestigious Justin Martyr School. The narrative begins with     
   Brian Aspinwall, a former student of Justin Martyr, who reflects on his experiences and on Prescott’s impact;        
   Aspinwall serves as narrator, giving insight into Prescott’s character and formative events. [7]                     
 • Prescott is portrayed as founding Justin Martyr School in the early 20th century and transforming it from a          
   struggling institution into a highly respected New England preparatory school. His dedication to discipline,         
   intellectual precision, and moral guidance earns admiration from students, faculty, and parents. [7]                 
 • The text mentions Prescott’s wife Harriet, who struggles with her role as headmaster’s wife and often feels          
   overshadowed by his devotion to the school. Tension between his family life—he has three daughters—and his commitment
   to Justin Martyr creates conflict. [7]                                                                               
 • Prescott’s interactions with students show his teaching methods and his influence as a mentor and father figure,     
   shaping their lives and guiding them toward success. At the same time, his strictness and unwavering commitment to   
   tradition create controversy and clashes with some students and faculty. [7]                                         
 • The summary indicates that the narrative jumps forward over time, highlighting major moments in Prescott’s tenure and
   the changing social landscape. It cites world events such as the Great Depression and World War II as affecting the  
   school and community. [7]                                                                                            
 • One question asks why Prescott is so unwavering in building a boys’ boarding school. The answer explains that        
   Prescott was educated at a boys’ boarding school founded by Horace, a religious fanatic who instilled discipline and 
   inspired his students. Prescott became close to Horace and promised to follow in his footsteps by establishing his   
   own boys’ boarding school. [8]                                                                                       
 • Cordelia is depicted as rebellious and bold, frequently challenging her father when she disagrees. She is angered by 
   his decision to focus on a boys’ school rather than programs to empower his daughters and other girls, viewing this  
   as favoritism toward men. The commentary notes that Cordelia even speculates her father might be gay because his     
   emotional focus seems to be on men and boys rather than women. [8]                                                   
 • A third question discusses why the school board forces Prescott to retire as headmaster. The answer stresses         
   Prescott’s strong attachment to the Episcopal school he built with his own resources and has led since its founding. 
   However, at age 82, his efficiency has declined, in movement and thinking, raising concerns among the board. The     
   board, having authority to remove the head, compels his retirement so that a younger, more energetic man named David 
   (described as taking over the position) can lead the school. [8]                                                     
